  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:4cRAkEHfXKo",
    "purpose": "Show how to clean, condition, oil and wax a two-year-old pair of leather Blundstone boots using Otterwax products, with a brief look at how the boots have held up.",
    "video_type": "how_to",
    "thesis": "After about two years of wear, the creator restores his Blundstones by cleaning, conditioning, oiling and waxing them in that order.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "The order is clean, condition, then wax, because conditioner and wax applied over dirt just make a mess.",
      "Cleaning means brushing off loose dirt with a horse hair brush, then scrubbing with saddle soap on a wet Tampico brush and wiping it off with a damp lint-free cloth.",
      "Salve is a deep, heavy moisturiser for dry, cracked leather. Leather oil is a lighter treatment that adds sheen, and the creator uses both.",
      "Wax is applied like the salve, left to dry, massaged in and buffed with horse hair to make the boots more water resistant.",
      "Wax and oils darken the leather, but it lightens again with wear.",
      "There is no fixed schedule. Treat the boots when they look or feel dry or stop beading water, and clean them regularly because dirt wears leather down.",
      "A Huckberry sponsor segment covers the Naglev Unico hiker: a seamless Kevlar upper, reinforced rand, wool sock liner, water resistant rather than waterproof, sold in EU sizes, with a suggestion to size up half a size."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
